# Copy to .env and fill in the secrets. Never commit .env.
#
# The connection settings below suit running the backend on the host (dev mode). With `docker compose up` the
# backend container ignores DB_URL, REDIS_HOST, REDIS_PORT, RABBITMQ_HOST, RABBITMQ_PORT, STORAGE_TYPE, STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR,
# MINIO_ENDPOINT and CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS: compose points it at the postgres, redis, rabbitmq and minio services and
# allows the origin http://localhost:${WEB_PORT} (PUBLIC_ORIGIN, at the end of this file, replaces that origin).
DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/csfaceit
DB_USER=csfaceit
DB_PASSWORD=csfaceit
REDIS_HOST=localhost
REDIS_PORT=6379
RABBITMQ_HOST=localhost
RABBITMQ_PORT=5672
# RabbitMQ credentials: the rabbitmq service is created with them and the backend logs in with them. The guest defaults
# are fine because compose publishes the broker on 127.0.0.1 only; choose your own before exposing it anywhere else.
RABBITMQ_USER=guest
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=guest
# Where the demo files are kept: "local" (files under STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR, relative to the backend's working directory)
# or "minio" (an S3-compatible server). Compose always runs the backend with "minio".
STORAGE_TYPE=local
STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=./data/storage
MINIO_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# MinIO credentials: the minio service is created with them and the backend logs in with them. The minioadmin defaults
# are fine because compose publishes MinIO on 127.0.0.1 only; choose your own before exposing it anywhere else.
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin
# Bucket the demo files go to; the backend creates it when it is missing.
MINIO_BUCKET=csfaceit
# MinIO server image of the compose stack (minio/minio is no longer published on Docker Hub). The default is the
# community build alpine/minio, pinned by tag and digest:
# MINIO_IMAGE=alpine/minio:RELEASE.2025-10-15T17-29-55Z@sha256:cf23643a6cf9ce159c57643ceb88279e431262282428c9e0bf3a7ef1a97e84b4
# The most a manual demo upload (POST /api/v1/matches/{id}/demo/upload) may be: a size with a unit, such as 1GB or 512MB.
# The Nginx of the compose stack accepts 1 GB for that path (frontend/nginx.conf); raise both to allow more.
DEMO_UPLOAD_MAX_SIZE=1GB
# Stored demo files older than this many days are deleted once a day (DemoRetentionJob); the parsed facts stay in the
# database and the demo keeps its status. 0 keeps the files forever.
DEMO_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# When that clean-up runs: Spring's six cron fields (second minute hour day month weekday), server time. Normally left alone.
# Keep the quotes: the value has spaces and asterisks, and `set -a && source .env` would split and expand it without them.
# DEMO_RETENTION_CRON="0 0 4 * * *"
# The most a recorder may upload for one clip (the video and the thumbnail each): a size with a unit, such as 1GB or
# 512MB. The Nginx of the compose stack accepts 1 GB for that path (frontend/nginx.conf); raise both to allow more.
CLIP_UPLOAD_MAX_SIZE=1GB
# A finished clip (done or failed) is deleted with its video and thumbnail after this many days. 0 keeps the clips forever.
CLIP_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# When that clean-up runs: Spring's six cron fields (second minute hour day month weekday), server time. Normally left alone.
# Keep the quotes: the value has spaces and asterisks, and `set -a && source .env` would split and expand it without them.
# CLIP_RETENTION_CRON="0 30 4 * * *"
# The most a demo may be once the parser has decompressed it, in bytes (default 2147483648, 2 GiB). A larger demo is
# marked FAILED as too large instead of being parsed.
PARSER_MAX_DEMO_BYTES=2147483648
# Secrets: leave empty here, set real values only in your local .env. FACEIT_API_KEY and APP_API_KEY are required:
# the backend refuses to start without FACEIT_API_KEY, and without APP_API_KEY every protected request is rejected.
FACEIT_API_KEY=
APP_API_KEY=
# The secret the recorder (the program that records the clips) sends in the X-Worker-Token header. While it is empty
# every request of a recorder is rejected and no clip is recorded. Choose a long random value, for example
# `openssl rand -hex 32`, and give the same one to the recorder. The token opens the recorder's API and nothing else,
# and the admin key does not open the recorder's API.
CLIP_WORKER_TOKEN=
# Signing in (see "Signing in" in the README). A visitor signs in with Steam, which needs no key and no registration. These
# are the SteamID64s (17 digits, comma separated) of the people who get the role ADMIN when they sign in; everybody else is
# a USER. Empty: nobody is an administrator. The role is in the answer of GET /api/v1/auth/me (the site does not show
# it) and does not yet let anyone act without the admin key (APP_API_KEY), which every protected request still needs.
AUTH_ADMIN_STEAM_IDS=
# The FACEIT OAuth application, for signing in with FACEIT (an application of the FACEIT Developer Portal; its redirect
# address has to be https). The sign-in with FACEIT itself is not written yet, so the two values are read and nothing
# more: only Steam is offered whether or not they are set. The secret is never logged: keep it out of the repository.
FACEIT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
FACEIT_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Password of the Grafana admin user (user name: admin). Needed only for `docker compose --profile observability up`,
# which starts Prometheus and Grafana; there is no default, and the grafana service stops with a message while it is
# empty. Choose your own, for example `openssl rand -hex 16`.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# FACEIT's Downloads API, the only way to get a demo file (the demo_url of a match is a private cloud address). It takes an
# access token of its own with the Downloads API scope, which FACEIT gives to an application that applies for it
# (https://fce.gg/downloads-api-application, an answer within 30 days); the Data API key above is refused with 403. With the
# token the import asks FACEIT for a signed address and downloads from that. Without it every import fails at once with the
# code DOWNLOAD_NEEDS_ACCESS and the page tells the visitor to upload the demo from the match's room on FACEIT. The token is
# never logged: keep it out of the repository.
FACEIT_DOWNLOADS_TOKEN=
# The address of the Downloads API; override it only to point the backend at a fake FACEIT.
# FACEIT_DOWNLOADS_URL=https://open.faceit.com/download/v2/demos/download
# FACEIT Data API address; override it only to point the backend at a fake FACEIT.
# FACEIT_BASE_URL=https://open.faceit.com/data/v4
# How far back a player's match history reaches (FACEIT answers only the last month unless asked for more).
# FACEIT_HISTORY_LOOKBACK=365d
# Only for the stack without a FACEIT key (docker-compose.fake.yml), whose recorder-fake makes a test pattern instead of
# playing the demo: the seconds of video it makes per second of real time. 1 is real time, so a clip of ten seconds
# takes ten; 0 is as fast as ffmpeg goes.
# FAKE_SPEED=1
# Only for the stack without a FACEIT key: the host port the fake FACEIT (and the fake Steam) is published on, and so the
# port of the address the browser is sent to when it signs in with the fake Steam.
# FAKE_FACEIT_PORT=8089
# Origins allowed to call the API from a browser (dev mode: the Vite dev server).
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# Host port the compose stack serves the web UI on.
WEB_PORT=3000
# The browser origin to allow when a proxy of your own in front of the web port terminates HTTPS: scheme and host (and
# port when it is not the default one), no path, such as https://example.com. Without it the backend allows only
# http://localhost:${WEB_PORT} and refuses every write the browser makes behind such a proxy (403). Not needed over
# plain HTTP on the web port, and not used with docker-compose.prod.yml, which allows https://${DOMAIN} itself.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://example.com
# Only for a server (docker-compose.prod.yml, see "Deploying on a server" in the README): the host name the site is
# served on, without https://, such as example.com. Its DNS A record must point at the server. Caddy gets the
# certificate for it and the backend allows https://${DOMAIN} as the origin. Required with docker-compose.prod.yml.
# While the server has no domain, `:80` in its place makes Caddy serve plain HTTP on port 80 without a certificate.
# DOMAIN=example.com
# On a server, also make every `docker compose` command in this directory use the production override, so that none of
# them can start the stack without it by mistake (the web port would be published on all interfaces again).
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.prod.yml
